    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">5</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.national_education_association"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">National Education Association</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2006-12-15</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: May 12, 2009
With 3.2 million members, the National Education Association is the nation's largest labor union. The Washington-based group is a network of mostly suburban and rural school systems. The N.E.A. wields considerable political clout, with politicians seeking its endorsement at election time.
Established in 1857, the N.E.A. broadened its base by merging with the National Association of School Superintendents and the American Normal School Association in 1870. The members of the organization, which is voluntary, includes dues-paying individuals as well as state and local associations of educators. The group's aim is to improve working conditions and salaries; the N.E.A. also sponsors research and promotes federal legislation.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
