  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N59348040560019237512">
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">4</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/organization_of_petroleum_exporting_countries_org"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">145</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-23</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries</skos:prefLabel>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.opec"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-09-08</nyt:first_use>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/organizations/o/organization_of_petroleum_exporting_countries/index.html"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: March 16, 2009
The Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries is an international oil cartel whose goal is to coordinate the petroleum policies of its 12 member states. In practice, OPEC members meet periodically to decide how to adjust their oil production in order to prop up prices or prevent them from collapsing -- or, most recently, to keep them falling further once they have collapsed.
In 2008, OPEC was tested by one of the steepest rollercoaster rides in oil prices in its history. Between 2001 and July of 2008, prices had risen, then soared, to a high of over $145 a barrel -- a trend that was washing huge amounts of wealth across the globe, into the coffers of OPEC members. But prices began to fall, then plunge, dropping to $32 a barrel by December 2008, and members of the group were in a panic. The sudden weakening of the global economy had been matched by a steep drop in demand for energy, and the prospect of worldwide recession through 2009 clouded oil's prospects even further. Even China, long the biggest engine of growth for oil demand, was cooling markedly.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:search_api_query rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://api.nytimes.com/svc/search/v1/article?query=+nytd_org_facet%3A%5BOrganization+of+Petroleum+Exporting+Countries%5D&amp;rank=newest&amp;fields=abstract,author,body,byline,classifiers_facet,column_facet,date,day_of_week_facet,des_facet,desk_facet,fee,geo_facet,lead_paragraph,material_type_facet,multimedia,nytd_byline,nytd_des_facet,nytd_geo_facet,nytd_lead_paragraph,nytd_org_facet,nytd_per_facet,nytd_section_facet,nytd_title,nytd_works_mentioned_facet,org_facet,page_facet,per_facet,publication_day,publication_month,publication_year,section_page_facet,small_image_height,small_image_url,small_image_width,source_facet,title,url,word_count,works_mentioned_facet</nyt:search_api_query>
