    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-18</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/united_automobile_workers_org"/>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/organizations/u/united_automobile_workers/index.html"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.united_auto_workers"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Nov. 3, 2009
The United Automobile Workers Union (U.A.W.) was founded in 1935 and is one of the largest unions in North America, claiming over 500,000 active members in sectors extending far beyond the auto industry. In the years after World War II it played a pivotal role in expanding the nation's labor movement, and the generous pension, health care and job security provisions it won helped define labor conditions for a generation.
But the shrinking of the North American auto industry beginning in the 1970s put the union on a steady downward path, and the crisis that engulfed the Big Three automakers in the fall of 2008 left it facing deeply difficult decisions. As of December 2008, the U.A.W.'s membership at G.M., Ford and Chrysler had declined to 139,000 workers, from 305,000 five years earlier, because of plant closings and a series of buyout and early-retirement programs.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-01-12</nyt:first_use>
  </rdf:Description>
