  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/39146794201836022552">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.airbus"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Sept. 25, 2009
 
Airbus is the aircraft manufacturing subsidiary of European Aeronautic Defense &amp; Space. Headquartered in Toulouse, France, it builds about half of the world's commercial airplanes.
Airbus began as a consortium of four national aircraft companies: DASA, the former aerospace subisiary of Daimler-Benz of Germany; Aérospatiale Matra of France; British Aerospace (which later became BAE Systems) and CASA of Spain. In the summer of 2000, the German, French and Spanish partners became a single company and a subsidiary of EADS, a publicly traded corporation. British Aerospace, which did not join EADS, retained a 20 percent share in Airbus until 2006, when it sold its stake to EADS.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:search_api_query rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://api.nytimes.com/svc/search/v1/article?query=+nytd_org_facet%3A%5BAirbus+Industrie%5D&amp;rank=newest&amp;fields=abstract,author,body,byline,classifiers_facet,column_facet,date,day_of_week_facet,des_facet,desk_facet,fee,geo_facet,lead_paragraph,material_type_facet,multimedia,nytd_byline,nytd_des_facet,nytd_geo_facet,nytd_lead_paragraph,nytd_org_facet,nytd_per_facet,nytd_section_facet,nytd_title,nytd_works_mentioned_facet,org_facet,page_facet,per_facet,publication_day,publication_month,publication_year,section_page_facet,small_image_height,small_image_url,small_image_width,source_facet,title,url,word_count,works_mentioned_facet</nyt:search_api_query>
