  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N27217737850798944712">
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Metropolitan Transportation Authority</skos:prefLabel>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/metropolitan_transportation_authority_org"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-30</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-09-05</nyt:first_use>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">0</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/organizations/m/metropolitan_transportation_authority/index.html"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.metropolitan_transportation_authority"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Dec. 15, 2009
The Metropolitan Transportation Authority was created in 1965 to operate what is now North America's largest transportation network.
In December 2009, the agency faced a sudden financial shortfall of more than $400 million and unveiled an austere budget that recommended the elimination of two lines and fewer subway trains running in the middle of the day, late at night and on weekends. In the previous spring, the agency had passed significant fare and toll increases, coupled with deep service cuts.
The authority operates New York City Transit buses and subways, the Metro North and Long Island railroads, Long Island Buses, and seven bridges and two tunnels in New York City. It serves 2.4 billion users annually and its reach touches 5,000 square miles in New York State and Connecticut.</skos:definition>
