  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N85037605523347970552">
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.united_parcel_service"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">United Parcel Service Inc</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">-- July 9, 2007 United Parcel Service began life in 1907 in Seattle as American Messenger Service, started, according to the company's official history, by a 19-year-old who had borrowed $100 to take advantage of the business opportunities created by a technological advance — the spread of the telephone. In 2007, the company describes itself as not only the world's largest deliverer of packages, but at "the frontiers of logistics, supply chain management, and e-Commerce." Its focus on high-tech efficiency was prompted in large measure by the rise of its greatest rival, FedEx; today, they dominate the domestic market for private parcel delivery. A mainstay in towns and cities across the country for decades, U.P.S. is most familiar for its brown trucks and brown uniforms, an identity is has taken to celebrating in slogans like "What Can Brown Do for You?"</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
