  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/42592627492991225052">
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">92</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/american_federation_of_labor_congress_of_industrial_organizations_org"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-03-02</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated April 14, 2009
The A.F.L.-C.I.O. is the nation's largest labor group, a federation of unions founded in 1955 by the merger of the American Federation of Labor and the Congress of Industrial Organizations. It claims 56 unions as members, which jointly represent about 11 million of the 15.7 million unionized workers in the United States. Its president, John J. Sweeney, has been the nation's top labor leader for 14 years. He is expected to step down in September 2009.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/organizations/a/american_federation_of_laborcongress_of_industrial_organizations/index.html"/>
