  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N66972066309787376882">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated May 15, 2009
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention is one of the 13 major operating components of the United States Department of Health and Human Services and a world authority on public health. The agency has helped discover AIDS, Legionnaires' disease, Ebola fever and Lassa fever. The C.D.C. dispenses public health information during disease outbreaks like the 2009 swine flu outbreak. To reassure and inform the public about the H1N1 virus, officials have made recommendations, held briefings and even used Twitter.
After the 2001 terrorist attacks and the anthrax attacks that shook public and professional confidence in the C.D.C., the agency became much more involved with bioterrorism and its possible effects on national security. Federal funds were used to upgrade disease surveillance systems, improve communication with local doctors and hospitals and bolster the ability of a network of laboratories throughout the country to detect the microbes considered most likely to be used in a bioterrorist attack.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/centers_for_disease_control_and_prevention_org"/>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/organizations/c/centers_for_disease_control_and_prevention/index.html"/>
