  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N21520201751612897382">
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2006-07-14</nyt:first_use>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/news/business/companies/ford_motor_company/index.html"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Ford Today
In early 2009, Ford emerged as the sole American automaker in a position to survive the steepest sales downturn in decades without a government bailout.
June 2009 sales figures included faint glimmers of hope for the industry and particularly for Ford, whose sales were down only 11 percent from June 2008. By contrast, sales fell 33 percent for General Motors and 42 percent for Chrysler.
On Nov. 2, Ford posted a surprise third-quarter profit of $997 million and said it had its first profitable quarter in North America in more than four years.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">369</nyt:associated_article_count>
