    <nyt:search_api_query rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://api.nytimes.com/svc/search/v1/article?query=+nytd_org_facet%3A%5BJetBlue+Airways%5D&amp;rank=newest&amp;fields=abstract,author,body,byline,classifiers_facet,column_facet,date,day_of_week_facet,des_facet,desk_facet,fee,geo_facet,lead_paragraph,material_type_facet,multimedia,nytd_byline,nytd_des_facet,nytd_geo_facet,nytd_lead_paragraph,nytd_org_facet,nytd_per_facet,nytd_section_facet,nytd_title,nytd_works_mentioned_facet,org_facet,page_facet,per_facet,publication_day,publication_month,publication_year,section_page_facet,small_image_height,small_image_url,small_image_width,source_facet,title,url,word_count,works_mentioned_facet</nyt:search_api_query>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">37</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2007-05-11</nyt:first_use>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.jetblue_airways"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">JetBlue Airways took to the skies in 2000 under a novel concept: bringing humanity back to air travel. Based at New York's Kennedy International Airport, JetBlue, a non-union airline, distinguished itself from other low-fare carriers such as Southwest Airlines by offering seat-back entertainment systems with live television, comfortable seats and blue corn chips. The airline quickly became a darling of the industry, earning millions of dollars in profits while big airlines lost money, and JetBlue's founder, David Neeleman, became a business celebrity.
JetBlue, which had emphasized its routes between New York and Florida, expanded to Boston and the West Coast as well as international destinations like Aruba, the Bahamas and the Dominican Republic. The airline's stock price soared and its earnings were strong for its first few years, yielding thousands of dollars in profit-sharing payments to its employees, and prompting rivals like Delta and United to launch their own low-fare carriers. JetBlue, which began flying with a fleet of Airbus A320 jets, added 100-seat regional jets from the Brazilian manufacturer Embraer in 2005 and began flying to more cities.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
