  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N64115965311573603812">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Overview
Updated June 10, 2009
Chrysler LLC, for years America's third-biggest automaker, ended a 42-day trip through bankruptcy court on Wednesday, June 10, 2009, by agreeing to an alliance with the Italian automaker Fiat.
The last obstacle to an exit â€” a temporary stay imposed by the Supreme Court â€” was lifted late Tuesday after the nine justices declined to hear a challenge of the deal by three Indiana state funds and several consumer groups.
Reeling from plummeting sales, by the end of 2008 the company needed an infusion of $4 billion from the federal government to stay afloat. In March 2009 President Obama ordered it to complete a planned partnership with Fiat within 30 days or face bankruptcy, after his car task force concluded that Chrysler could not survive as a stand-alone company. Days before the April 30 deadline the United Automobile Workers reached a deal on the financing of their health benefits, and the company's major creditors agreed to convert their debt into equity. But last-minute negotiations with smaller bondholders failed, leading to the bankruptcy announcement.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
