  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/74326256924009431042">
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated July 29, 2009
Founded in 1985, America Online, grew to become the dominant Internet service during the late 1990s, when most Americans first started using e-mail and the Internet.
As the companyâ€™s prominence and stock market value rose, it made a series of acquisitions including rival Compuserve, browser maker Netscape, and ultimately the giant media firm Time Warner.
Shortly after the $165 billion acquisition, announced in 2000, was completed in 2001 AOLâ€™s growth started to slow. First its advertising revenue was hit by the end of the dot-com boom. Then the company was hurt by the rise of fast Internet service offered by cable and phone companies that was much faster than the dial-up service AOL specialized in. It peaked at 27 million subscribers in late 2002.</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/news/business/companies/aol/index.html"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2007-09-18</nyt:first_use>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-28</nyt:latest_use>
