  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N29207218196461380622">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The Holy Land Foundation for Relief and Development was once the nation's largest Islamic charity. In 2001, the group and five of its backers were indicted on what became the government?s largest and most complex legal effort to shut down what it contends is American financing for terrorist organizations in the Middle East. In 2007, a lengthy trial ended in a mistrial. But on Nov. 24, 2008, a second trial turned out far differently, with guilty verdicts on all 108 counts.
President Bush announced he was freezing the charity?s assets in December 2001, saying that the radical Islamic group Hamas had ?obtained much of the money it pays for murder abroad right here in the United States.?</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.holy_land_foundation_for_relief_and_development"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2008-11-26</nyt:latest_use>
