  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/81948933336555015292">
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The subprime mortgage meltdown made 2007 a disastrous year for Bear Stearns, one of the nationâ€™s largest underwriters of mortgage bonds. Beginning this summer with the housing slowdown, Bear Stearns has stood as the prime example of how Wall Streetâ€™s big bet on securities based on risky home loans went south.
While many of its peers, including Merrill, Morgan Stanley and Citigroup, have announced far more in devaluations, Bear draws far more of its profit from its trading operations. That was reflected in its fixed income unit, which reported a net loss of $1.5 billion, down sharply from the $1.1 billion in profit the bank reported for the same time last year.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">183</nyt:associated_article_count>
