    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Rocky Mountain News</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-01-10</nyt:first_use>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/organizations/r/rocky_mountain_news/index.html"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">2</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The Rocky Mountain News in Denver ceased publication on Feb. 27, 2009.
E.W. Scripps, which owned the newspaper, known locally as The Rocky, failed to find a buyer for the daily. Scripps's chief executive, Rich Boehne, said in a statement that The Rocky was "a victim of changing times in our industry and huge economic challenges." Scripps said the newspaper lost $16 million in 2008.
The Rocky had about 230 reporters, editors and other employees in its newsroom. It closed just two months short of its 150th anniversary.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.rocky_mountain_news"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-10-12</nyt:latest_use>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/rocky_mountain_news_org"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
  </rdf:Description>
