    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Fairfield Greenwich Group</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: April 2, 2009
Investment Securities, the Ponzi scheme Mr. Madoff operated, which may have been the largest in history. Mr. Madoff, whose fraud amounted to a reported $65 billion, pleaded guilty in March 2009. Bernard L. Madoff The Fairfield Greenwich Group, a investment firm founded in 1983, poured billions of dollars into
Massachusetts regulators have sued Fairfield Greenwich, one of the earliest of so-called feeder fund managers, for fraud, saying it had repeatedly misled investors about how diligently it checked out Mr. Madoff's operations over the years. Walter M. Noel Jr., a founder of the firm, his partner Jeffrey H. Tucker and his son-in-law Andrés Piedrahita were also among the fund managers hit with a temporary asset freeze imposed March 31, 2009, by a state judge in Connecticut.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/fairfield_greenwich_group_org"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">2</nyt:number_of_variants>
  </rdf:Description>
