    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Boeing Co</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/news/business/companies/boeing_company/index.html"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Oct. 9, 2009
The word "big" does not begin to describe Boeing, a global aviation leader and the world's second largest military contractor. Nearly 12,000 Boeing jetliners command the skies, from the small and popular 737 to the massive two-level 747, the world's first superjumbo jet. Boeing's military lineup includes a fleet of cargo planes, Chinook and other helicopters and the F-22 Raptor, the newest, fastest and most expensive fighter jet. And if someone put a sticker on the International Space Station, Boeing could claim a spot there, too.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.boeing"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">297</nyt:associated_article_count>
