  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N91033351149978686012">
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/organizations/b/baath_party/index.html"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-19</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">3</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Iraqâ€™s Baath Party ruled as a dictatorship for 35 years, mostly under Saddam Hussein. Most members were Sunni Muslims. Tens of thousands of Iraqis died or were persecuted by the party; it was outlawed after the American invasion in 2003. In December 2008, up to 35 officials in the Iraqi Ministry of the Interior, ranking as high as general, were arrested, some of them accused of quietly working to reconstitute the party, according to senior security officials in Baghdad.
The Baath Party was Mr. Hussein's political base, but it grew to be much more. The party was one element of a three-sided security apparatus that kept Iraqis cowed. The other two were the army and the Mukhabarat secret service.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/baath_party_org"/>
