    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">3</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/organizations/i/international_olympic_committee/index.html"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-19</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">International Olympic Committee</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Oct. 2, 2009
is the organization that runs the Olympic Games. It was created in 1894, in advance of the first Olympics of the modern era, which took place in Athens two years later. One of its main responsibilities is to decide where the summer and winter games will be held. International Olympic Committee The
Countries compete vigorously to secure the right to play host to the Olympics, a practice that led to a series of scandals. Officials involved in securing the 2002 winter games for Salt Lake City later admitted to spending $3 million in bribes and illicit payments to win the games, and 10 I.O.C. delegates resigned or were dismissed as a result. In Japan, an investigation found that millions had been spent on entertainment of I.O.C. delegates, including items that were described as illegitimate.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">218</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/international_olympic_committee_org"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-09-09</nyt:first_use>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
  </rdf:Description>
