    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/al_shabab_org"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/organizations/s/al-shabab/index.html"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Al-Shabab</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2008-11-13</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Nov. 24, 2009 Â 
Al Shabab is an Islamic organization that controls much of southern Somalia, excluding the capital, Mogadishu. It has waged an insurgency against Somalia's transitional government and its Ethiopian supporters since 2006. Originally the militant wing of the Islamic Courts Union, the group that controlled Somalia prior to the country's invasion by Ethiopian forces, Shabab leaders have claimed affiliation with Al Qaeda since 2007.
On Nov. 23, 2009, United States officials unsealed terrorism-related charges against men they say were key actors in a recruitment effort that led roughly 20 young Americans to join Shabab.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.hizbul_shabaab"/>
