    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The Los Angeles County Museum of Art, one of the largest cultural institutions in Southern California, is young by the standards of most major museums. Started in 1910 as part of the city?s Museum of History, Science and Art, it became an independent institution, devoted exclusively to the visual arts, only in 1961. Its original three-building facility opened on Wilshire Boulevard in Hancock Park in 1967. While Lacma, as the museum is known, considers itself an encyclopedic museum, it is especially known for its collection of Asian art ? including one of the largest collections of Korean art outside of Korea ? and for its collections of the art of Central and South America, from the pre-Columbian era to modern artists including Diego Rivera and Frida Kahlo.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-11-15</nyt:first_use>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
  </rdf:Description>
