    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Decades after Brian Wilson formed the Beach Boys, it's hard to say anything about them that they haven't already contradicted themselves. Once surfin' pin-ups, they remade themselves as avant-garde pop artists, then psychedelic oracles. After that they were down-home hippies, then retro-hip icons. Eventually they devolved into none of the above: a kind of perpetual-motion nostalgia machine.
? Peter Ames Carlin Still, the Beach Boys remain one of the most beloved of American pop bands. A perennial favorite on radio, the group was inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame in 1988.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-09-12</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Beach Boys</skos:prefLabel>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/beach_boys_org"/>
  </rdf:Description>
