  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N74573722350947571472">
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Sun Microsystems Inc</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/news/business/companies/sun_microsystems_inc/index.html"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: April 20, 2009
Sun is one of the largest sellers of server computers and is known for systems based on its Sparc chips. It has a vast software portfolio, including the Solaris operating system, the open-source MySQL database and the Java programming language.
ended its talks with Sun. The Sun board balked at that deal after I.B.M. lowered its offer to $9.40 a share from $10. The deal with Oracle represented a 42 percent premium over Sun's closing price of $6.69 on April 17. I.B.M. , the technology information company, said it would acquire Sun Microsystems. The agreement with Oracle came about two weeks after  Oracle Corporation On April 20, 2009, the</skos:definition>
