  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N89392341534493966472">
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">4</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated June 15, 2009
Fiat, the iconic Italian automaker, bounced bank from near-bankruptcy a scant five years ago to become a new force on the global auto scene when it took over Chrysler LLC, formerly America's third-biggest car company. Fiat was given management control over Chrysler and 20 percent of its stock (an amount that will eventually grow to 35 percent) as part of an alliance completed on June 10, 2009 ending Chrysler's 42-day trip through bankruptcy court.
The last obstacle to an exit - a temporary stay imposed by the Supreme Court - was lifted after the nine justices declined to hear a challenge of the deal by three Indiana state funds and several consumer groups.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/fiat_spa_org"/>
