    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Joint Chiefs of Staff</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-08-28</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated May 12, 2009
The Joint Chiefs of Staff is a board that serves as the unified high command of the American military, and its chairman is the highest ranking military commander. He also serves as the principal military advisor to the President, Secretary of Defense, and the National Security Council. The board consists of the chiefs of the four branches of the U.S. armed forces.
The concept of a board comprised of the heads of the various military branches was first developed after criticism arose about the lack of coordinated military action in the Spanish American War in 1898. Such a board existed in several forms over the first half of the 20th century. The Joint Chiefs of Staff was developed in 1942, and the National Security Act of 1947 established it formally.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-10-23</nyt:first_use>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">36</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.joint_chiefs_of_staff"/>
