  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/10331225672768371902">
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.take-two_interactive"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/take_two_interactive_software_inc_org"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Take-Two Interactive Software Inc</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-23</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">13</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Like players of many video games, the makers of game consoles are themselves engaged in a fierce contest. The combatants ? Sony, Microsoft and Nintendo ? are competing to be the center of interactive entertainment. Their relative success has been in considerable flux.
Sony, maker of the PlayStation consoles, jumped out to a substantial lead with the PlayStation 2, which was introduced in 2000. Microsoft, which had entered the fray with its Xbox console in 2001, lagged in market share, as did Nintendo?s GameCube.
But fortunes started to change with the release of the latest generation of game consoles. In November 2005, Microsoft launched the Xbox 360, hoping to get out to an early lead in the new console cycle. And it did. By a year later, it had shipped around 10 million consoles worldwide.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/subjects/c/computer_and_video_games/index.html"/>
