  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/40347214486200581812">
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/organizations/n/new_school_university/index.html"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2007-01-07</nyt:first_use>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">25</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">2</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated May 8, 2009
The New School, founded in 1919, was the brainchild of the historian Charles Beard and the philosopher John Dewey. At first it offered courses that addressed the educational, political, social and economic issues of the day.
The university gradually rose in stature in the 1930s and 1940s when it welcomed social scientists fleeing persecution in Germany and other countries.
On May 6, 2009, the university's current president, Bob Kerrey, announced that he would step down when his contract expires on July 1, 2011. The announcement followed months of criticism by faculty and students.</skos:definition>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">New School</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-09-22</nyt:latest_use>
