  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N21991099516331623202">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-01-08</nyt:first_use>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/organizations/l/liberation_tigers_of_tamil_eelam/index.html"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">133</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-02</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: May 18, 2009
, began fighting for a separate Tamil homeland in 1983, with the group's first assault coming in an ambush of government troops. When subsequent anti-Tamil riots left hundreds dead, the battle was fully joined. An estimated 75,000 people have been killed in the violence. Vellupillai Prabhakaran The Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam, or Tamil Tigers, a Sri Lankan rebel group led by
After decades of bloodletting, the government's war with the Tamil Tigers may be over. The Sri Lankan authorities declared a final victory in their 26-year war with the Tamil Tigers on May 18, saying in a state television broadcast that the leader of the insurgents was among 250 fighters killed in a final battle for the last sliver of rebel-held territory.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/liberation_tigers_of_tamil_eelam_org"/>
