  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/31215860869138851412">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: March 20, 2009
By Jon Caramanica
Fleetwood Mac, in its golden years, seems to cater to two different constituencies: Lindsey Buckingham, with indulgent guitar playing and general air of preening, tries to keep a low-burning flame lit. Stevie Nicks, his former lover, appears content to be laurel-resting, coasting on a blend of shawls, twirls, and, occasionally, piercingly cloying vocals (a screen scrolling words -- lyrics presumably -- at her feet). The rhythm section -- the drummer Mick Fleetwood and the bass player John McVie, for whom, together, the band is named -- soldier on like exceedingly tolerant parents. Mr. Fleetwood, ponytail intact, plays with force, if not grace, and Mr. McVie succeeds for not drawing notice. In a band so obsessed with role playing, such restraint qualifies as innovation.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.fleetwood_mac"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-03-21</nyt:latest_use>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">3</nyt:associated_article_count>
