  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N34004153237481572262">
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Cooper-Hewitt National Design Museum</skos:prefLabel>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/cooper_hewitt_national_design_museum_org"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The Cooper-Hewitt National Design Museum started out in 1897 as part of the Cooper Union, the noted college for art, architecture and engineering in Greenwich Village. The museum's founders -- Amy, Eleanor and Sarah Hewitt -- were granddaughters of the school's founder, Peter Cooper. Now housed in the landmark Andrew Carnegie Mansion on Fifth Avenue, the Cooper-Hewitt is the nation's only institution devoted exclusively to historic and contemporary design. It became a branch of the Smithsonian Institution in 1967.
The museum's collection of 250,000 design objects spans 24 centuries, with pieces ranging from one-of-a-kind to mass-produced items. The holdings are spread among the museum's archives, library and four curatorial departments: product design and decorative arts; drawings, prints and graphic design; textiles; and wall coverings. The design library serves as a resource for students, scholars, contemporary designers and collectors.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.cooper_hewitt_national_design_museum"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-10-29</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
