  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N53734689449362397522">
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-08-18</nyt:latest_use>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.washington_mutual"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An upstart bank created in 1889 to help the city of Seattle rebuild its downtown after a devastating fire grew into Washington Mutual, the largest American savings and loan. But if its rise was storied, its fall was spectacular, ending in the largest bank failure in American history to date.
The thrift was originally known for its ability to adopt, and adapt to, consumer and technological innovations. It survived economic busts, including the 1929 crash, because it had piled up assets in the flush periods before.
Read More...
In the 1970?s, Washington Mutual claims to have pioneered the nation?s first shared cash machine network, and, along with a group of other savings banks, helped set up the first Pay-by-Phone banking network .</skos:definition>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Washington Mutual Inc</skos:prefLabel>
