    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/organizations/g/group_of_20/index.html"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.g20_developing_nations"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">66</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Group of Twenty</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Sept. 28, 2009  |  Latest Developments
Updated: Sept. 25, 2009
The Group of 20, or G-20, is an international body that meets to discuss economic issues. Its members -- 19 countries with some of the world's biggest industrial and emerging economies, plus the European Union -- represent about 90 percent of the world's gross national product, 80 percent of world trade (including trade within the European Union) and two-thirds of the global population. The G-20, which represents a far broader range of the world's industrialized economies than the more Atlantic-oriented G-8, has taken on a new prominence in the current economic crisis.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-15</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2008-11-14</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_org"/>
