  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N35622668938673602102">
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-02</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-01-10</nyt:first_use>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/federal_reserve_bank_of_new_york_org"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The Federal Reserve system consists of 12 regional banks and a board of governors, based in Washington. Among the regional banks, the Federal Reserve Bank of New York, or the New York Fed, as it is generally known, has always been first among equals. Its jurisdiction over Wall Street makes it the largest of the Fed banks in terms of assets and volume of activity. As the credit crisis developed in 2007 and 2008, Timothy F. Geithner, then the president of the New York Fed, played a key role in the rescue of a string of financial giants, including Bear Stearns and A.I.G., and in the decision to let Lehman Brothers fail. In January 2009 Mr. Geithner became President Obama's Treasury Secretary, and was succeeded by William Dudley, a former Goldman Sachs economist who had been executive vice president of the bank's Fed's markets group, a post in which he oversaw the bank's trading operations.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
